u_{\mu}$ process is measured using proton-proton collision data collected by the LHCb experiment at a center-of-mass energy of 13 TeV, corresponding to an integrated luminosity of 3 fb$^{-1}$. The analysis focuses on the angular distribution of the $D_s^{*-}$ decay products, specifically the angle between the $D_s^-$ and the $D_s^{*-}$ in the $D_s^{*-}$ rest frame. This angular distribution is utilized to determine the shape of the differential decay rate. The findings are compared with predictions derived from the Standard Model, employing both the perturbative QCD approach and the light-cone sum rule method. The results indicate that the differential decay rate is consistent with the perturbative QCD prediction across the entire kinematic range, whereas the light-cone sum rule prediction aligns with the data only at low values of the squared momentum transfer, $q^2$. Furthermore, the differential decay rate is found to be consistent with the perturbative QCD prediction for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\ell^{+}
